% What a record holds, and what putting a page out of it takes, over a
% job of more than one page.
%
% run-page-lifetime-test.sh runs this file once through the device that
% writes down what it was asked to paint and once through a device that
% paints as it goes, and holds the two to the same bytes page for page.
% What it is for is three quantities no comparison of pages can see,
% because a record whose lifetime is the job's rather than the page's
% paints exactly the right pixels:
%
%   What a record holds. A page ends, and what the record describes ends
%   with it. A record not given up at that boundary holds every page a
%   job has drawn so far and replays all of them, once per band, to
%   paint the last of them -- and the page still comes out right,
%   because each page opens by painting over its predecessors.
%
%   What putting a page out takes. The raster a record is played into
%   comes out of the half of virtual memory nothing here gives back, so
%   a raster per page leaves every page's rows behind and a job of n
%   pages ends holding n pages. The device that paints directly is the
%   control: it holds one page whatever the job does, and is flat.
%
%   How many times an image is played. An image is the one entry whose
%   replay costs the picture rather than a mark, and a row written twice
%   carries what one write left -- so an image played again over rows it
%   has already been played into leaves the page it left the first time.
%   What is claimed is that a band replay plays an image once for each
%   band that reaches it, and that the marks the walk steps over on the
%   way to it do not enter.
%
% The multi-page job is run twice: once with the budget the class
% carries, which covers a page of this size and so puts it out whole,
% and once with a budget that divides it into bands. The two are
% different routes through the emission and a raster kept from one page
% to the next has to be put back to what Create left it as on both of
% them, so both are asked for the same bytes and the same memory.
%
% Every quantity is printed rather than judged here, and the wrapper
% judges. Two of them are only asked of the recording device, since a
% device that paints has no record and no band loop; the memory is asked
% of both, which is what makes the flat answer mean something.

/W 240 def
/H 480 def
/ROWBYTES W 3 mul def
/NPAGES 4 def
% Forty rows to a band, so the banded case divides the page into twelve
% of them and where a mark falls among the bands is stated rather than
% hoped for.
/BANDROWS 40 def
/BANDBYTES ROWBYTES BANDROWS mul def
/NBANDS H BANDROWS add 1 sub BANDROWS idiv def

% -  bands?  bool
% Whether this run's page arrives a band at a time, asked of the
% interpreter rather than read off the device.
%
% The smallest band there is is asked for and what became of the page is
% read back through currentsystemparams: a device that paints holds its
% page whole whatever budget is set on it, and a record holds it in
% bands. What the budget was is put back, so asking changes nothing --
% the cases below set their own.
%
% Asked rather than inferred from a method the device happens to carry,
% because the presence of a band loop and the use of one are different
% facts: a record whose budget buys every row of the page holds that
% page whole, and only the answer can say so.
/bands? {
    2 dict begin
    /b.had DEVICE /.bandbytes known def
    /b.was b.had { DEVICE /.bandbytes get }{ 0 } ifelse def
    DEVICE /.bandbytes 1 put
    currentsystemparams /CurBandHeight get 0 gt
    b.had { DEVICE /.bandbytes b.was put }
          { DEVICE /.bandbytes undef } ifelse
    end
} bind def

% Which run this is, and it is the run that answers.
/banding bands? def
/tag banding { (rec) }{ (dir) } ifelse def
/ID 1183615869 internaldict def

/join { % a b  .  ab
    /jn.b exch store /jn.a exch store
    jn.a length jn.b length add string
    dup 0 jn.a putinterval
    dup jn.a length jn.b putinterval
} bind def
/num { 20 string cvs } bind def
/sp { ( ) print } bind def
/nl { (\n) print } bind def

% The name both runs write beside each other under, so the wrapper can
% compare them page for page.
/pagename (page-%d-) tag join (.ppm) join def

% How much of global virtual memory the run has taken. Raster memory is
% not part of virtual memory as a program sees it (PLRM 3.7.3), but it
% is taken from the global half -- the half a restore does not rewind
% and the half nothing here gives back -- so a raster left behind is
% what this reads.
/gvm { globalvmstatus pop exch pop } bind def

% Automatic collection is turned off for the whole of this file, because
% what it reads is memory NOT being given back.
%
% Every assertion below is of the form "the job did not grow", so a
% collection falling inside the measured stretch does not make this file
% fail -- it makes it PASS. The raster a page left behind is exactly
% what a collection reclaims, and reclaiming it is what this is written
% to notice the absence of. A run that collects between the readings
% reports every route flat and holds nothing to anything, which is the
% shape a leak gate fails silently in.
%
% The meter below is the other half of the same care: it takes a known
% quantity and requires the reading to see it, so that a reading which
% cannot see memory at all is told apart from a route that takes none.
% Together they say the instrument works and that nothing is quietly
% undoing what it measures.
%
% PLRM C.3.5 provides for this: automatic collection may be disabled,
% and -2 is the operand for both banks (PLRM 8.2 vmreclaim).
-2 vmreclaim

% A device row as a user-space y.
/dy { H exch sub } bind def

% ---- the meter, before anything is measured with it ----
% A reading that could not see memory being taken would report every
% route flat and pass everything. So a known quantity is taken in the
% half being read and the difference is printed, for the wrapper to hold
% against what was asked for. It is more than a page of this size costs
% to hold, so a meter that passes here can see a raster left behind.
%
% Taken as several strings because one of them cannot be that large: a
% composite object's length is held in a sixteen-bit field in the
% narrower of the two builds, so a string past that is refused.
/METERCHUNK 60000 def
/METERN 6 def
/METERBYTES METERCHUNK METERN mul def
/g0 gvm def
currentglobal true setglobal
/meterhold METERN array def
0 1 METERN 1 sub { meterhold exch METERCHUNK string put } for
setglobal
(METER ) print METERBYTES num print sp gvm g0 sub num print nl

(PAGE ) print W num print sp H num print nl
(BANDS ) print NBANDS num print nl

% -  paintpage  -
% One page's drawing: fills, a stroke lying across a band boundary, and
% text, which is where the coverage-weighted blends come from. The same
% drawing on every page, so that a record holding one page's worth and a
% record holding the whole job's differ by the page number alone.
%
% The bar across the top is on the first rows of the page, which is the
% first band of the banded case. A raster standing for a second page
% starts that band from the rows it left the first page's last band on,
% so a page whose first band is nothing but the ground would never ask
% whether those rows had been put back to what Create left them as.
/paintpage { % -  .  -
    gsave
        0.1 0.7 0.3 setrgbcolor
        newpath 10 5 dy moveto 230 5 dy lineto
                230 30 dy lineto 10 30 dy lineto closepath fill
    grestore
    gsave
        0.2 0.4 0.6 setrgbcolor
        newpath 20 40 dy moveto 220 40 dy lineto
                220 120 dy lineto 20 120 dy lineto closepath fill
    grestore
    gsave
        0 setgray 1 setlinewidth
        newpath 20 200 dy moveto 220 260 dy lineto stroke
    grestore
    % Text at five heights rather than one. A glyph reaches a record as
    % one mark and not as one per inked pixel, so a page carrying a
    % single line of it holds too few marks for a record accumulating
    % across pages to be told from one that is not.
    gsave
        0 setgray
        /Times-Roman findfont 18 scalefont setfont
        [ 290 310 330 350 370 ] {
            20 exch dy moveto (Page lifetime) show
        } forall
    grestore
    gsave
        0.9 0.1 0.1 setrgbcolor
        newpath
        30 380 dy moveto 210 380 dy lineto
        210 440 dy lineto 30 440 dy lineto closepath
        70 395 dy moveto 70 425 dy lineto
        170 425 dy lineto 170 395 dy lineto closepath
        eofill
    grestore
} bind def

% label banded  lifecase  -
% A job of NPAGES identical pages, saying after each of them what the
% record holds and what the run has taken.
/lifecase { % label banded  .  -
    /lc.banded exch store
    /lc.label exch store
    << /PageSize [W H] /OutputFile pagename >> setpagedevice
    banding lc.banded and { DEVICE /.bandbytes BANDBYTES put } if
    1 1 NPAGES {
        /lc.pg exch store
        paintpage
        banding {
            DEVICE ID /.recordcost get exec pop pop /lc.marks exch store
            (COST ) print lc.label print sp lc.pg num print sp
            lc.marks num print nl
        } if
        showpage
        % read where the page has been put out, so that the raster it was
        % played into is memory this run has taken
        (GVM ) print lc.label print sp lc.pg num print sp gvm num print nl
    } for
} bind def

(whole) false lifecase
(band) true lifecase

% ---- and the same job with a save and its restore around each page ----
% Wrapping a save and its restore around each page is the ordinary way to
% write a job (data/device.ps), and virtual memory goes back at the
% restore to what the save found. What a record paints through is not
% virtual memory -- it is a raster (PLRM 3.7.3) -- but the name the
% record reaches it by is, so that name has to be older than the save.
% One written inside a save is taken away at the restore, the page after
% builds a raster of its own, and the raster before it is left where
% nothing can find it and nothing can give it up.
%
% No page can see any of that: each one is painted correctly, in a raster
% of its own. Two readings can. The device the record is painting through
% must be the same device after every page of the job, since a second
% device is a second raster; and the memory a raster comes out of must
% not grow from page to page, which is the same meter the job above is
% read with. Both are asked only of the recording run, a device that
% paints having no raster underneath it to keep.
%
% The pages are written too, and the wrapper holds them to the pages the
% device that paints wrote, so a raster kept when it should have been
% rebuilt -- another page, another size -- is caught by the bytes.
/NSAVED 3 def

% -  savedcase  -
/savedcase { % -  .  -
    << /PageSize [W H] /OutputFile pagename >> setpagedevice
    banding { DEVICE /.bandbytes BANDBYTES put } if
    /sc.dev null store
    /sc.mem 0 store
    /sc.bad false store
    % Before any page at all, and so before anything the job opens: the
    % record is already painting through a device. That is the whole of
    % why one raster serves the job -- a raster taken up at the first
    % page is taken up inside whatever the job has opened by then, and
    % the name it is reached by goes back at the restore.
    banding {
        DEVICE /.state get /.playdev known not {
            (FAIL the record is painting through nothing before its ) print
            (first page, so the raster it will paint through is taken up ) print
            (inside whatever the job has opened by then\n) print
            /sc.bad true store
        } if
    } if
    % One page before the measured ones, and outside any save. How deep a
    % band is is asked for after the device has been made, there being no
    % other way to ask, so the raster the device started on is not the
    % one this job wants and the first page is where it is exchanged.
    % Exchanging it inside a save is a restore taking the exchange back,
    % which is a thing of its own and not what is being read below.
    paintpage
    showpage
    1 1 NSAVED {
        /sc.pg exch store
        save
        paintpage
        showpage
        restore
        banding {
            DEVICE /.state get /.playdev known not {
                (FAIL after page ) print sc.pg num print
                ( the record is painting through nothing: the restore) print
                ( took away the name its raster was reached by\n) print
                /sc.bad true store
            }{
                sc.dev null eq {
                    /sc.dev DEVICE /.state get /.playdev get store
                    /sc.mem gvm store
                }{
                    DEVICE /.state get /.playdev get sc.dev eq not {
                        (FAIL page ) print sc.pg num print
                        ( was painted through a different device from the) print
                        ( page before it, so a raster was built for it\n) print
                        /sc.bad true store
                    } if
                    gvm sc.mem gt {
                        (FAIL the job grew by ) print gvm sc.mem sub num print
                        ( bytes over page ) print sc.pg num print
                        (, which is where a raster left behind shows\n) print
                        /sc.bad true store
                    } if
                } ifelse
            } ifelse
        } if
    } for
    banding sc.bad not and {
        (OK   one raster painted ) print NSAVED num print
        ( pages, each inside its own save and restore\n) print
    } if
} bind def

savedcase

% ---- and the same, with the band grid asked for before the device ----
% A raster is exchanged where the band grid it was built for is not the
% one in force, and the case above puts that exchange outside a save on
% purpose. This one asks for the band grid before the device exists -- of
% the class the device is made from, which is the only way to have a
% record built on the grid it is going to keep -- so nothing is exchanged
% and everything the raster takes up for the job it takes up while the
% job is still being set up.
%
% What that reaches is the second half of a raster's state. A device
% takes up what it needs to move its rows from one run to the next at its
% first move (data/image.ps), and a first move made while a page is being
% put out is made inside whatever the job has opened: the restore takes
% the take-up back, and the move after it finds the rows the device gave
% up rather than the ones it built. What comes of that is a page painted
% into rows holding no pixel, which is a page and not a count -- so what
% says so is the wrapper holding these pages to the pages the device that
% paints wrote.
/NBUILT 3 def

% -  builtcase  -
/builtcase { % -  .  -
    /bc.dev null store
    /bc.bad false store
    % The band grid, asked of the class the device is about to be made
    % from and put back the moment it has been. A device reads it when it
    % is made and every page after; asking it of the device -- which is
    % what every other case here does, there being no other way once the
    % device exists -- is asking after the raster has been built on some
    % other grid, and the first page is then where one raster is
    % exchanged for another.
    banding {
        .privatedict /.xpost_RECORDDEVICE 2 copy known {
            get /bc.rdev exch store
            /bc.was bc.rdev /.bandbytes get store
            bc.rdev /.bandbytes BANDBYTES put
        }{
            pop pop
            (FAIL the recording class cannot be reached, so nothing here ) print
            (can ask for a band grid before the device exists\n) print
            /bc.bad true store
        } ifelse
    } if
    << /PageSize [W H] /OutputFile pagename >> setpagedevice
    banding bc.bad not and { bc.rdev /.bandbytes bc.was put } if
    % and the device really was built on it, since a case that quietly
    % held its page whole would exercise none of what it is here for
    banding bc.bad not and {
        DEVICE /.state get /.playrows get BANDBYTES ROWBYTES idiv ne {
            (FAIL the record was not built on the band grid asked for, so ) print
            (this case is not the case it says it is\n) print
            /bc.bad true store
        } if
    } if
    1 1 NBUILT {
        /bc.pg exch store
        save
        paintpage
        showpage
        restore
        banding {
            DEVICE /.state get /.playdev known not {
                (FAIL after page ) print bc.pg num print
                ( the record built on its own band grid is painting ) print
                (through nothing\n) print
                /bc.bad true store
            }{
                bc.dev null eq
                    { /bc.dev DEVICE /.state get /.playdev get store }
                    { DEVICE /.state get /.playdev get bc.dev eq not {
                        (FAIL page ) print bc.pg num print
                        ( was painted through a different device from the) print
                        ( page before it\n) print
                        /bc.bad true store
                      } if }
                ifelse
            } ifelse
        } if
    } for
    banding bc.bad not and {
        (OK   one raster, built on the band grid it kept, painted ) print
        NBUILT num print ( saved and restored pages\n) print
    } if
} bind def

builtcase

% ---- the image, and what the marks in front of it cost it ----
% The same page twice, differing only in how many marks stand between
% the start of the record and the image. A band replay steps over the
% marks that do not reach the band it is filling, and what is claimed is
% that stepping over them costs nothing: the image is played once for
% each band that reaches it either way.
%
% The image is placed across the middle of the page, so that it reaches
% some of the bands and not others. A picture covering every band would
% be played the same number of times by a replay that played it once per
% band and by one that played it once per page.
/imagepage { % nfills  .  -
    /ip.n exch store
    0 1 ip.n 1 sub {
        /ip.i exch store
        gsave 0 setgray
            newpath 5 ip.i 5 mul 5 add dy moveto
                    40 ip.i 5 mul 5 add dy lineto
                    40 ip.i 5 mul 8 add dy lineto
                    5 ip.i 5 mul 8 add dy lineto closepath fill
        grestore
    } for
    gsave
        /DeviceGray setcolorspace
        60 340 dy translate
        160 240 scale
        << /ImageType 1 /Width 8 /Height 8 /BitsPerComponent 8
           /Decode [0 1] /ImageMatrix [8 0 0 -8 0 8]
           /DataSource <00204060 80a0c0e0 10305070 90b0d0f0
                        00204060 80a0c0e0 10305070 90b0d0f0
                        00204060 80a0c0e0 10305070 90b0d0f0
                        00204060 80a0c0e0 10305070 90b0d0f0> >> image
    grestore
} bind def

% label nfills  imagecase  -
% Put out one such page and say what it cost. The page device is made
% again for each case, so the count of plays each reports is its own.
/imagecase { % label nfills  .  -
    /ic.n exch store
    /ic.label exch store
    << /PageSize [W H] /OutputFile pagename >> setpagedevice
    banding { DEVICE /.bandbytes BANDBYTES put } if
    ic.n imagepage
    banding {
        DEVICE ID /.recordcost get exec pop pop /ic.marks exch store
    } if
    showpage
    banding {
        (PLAYS ) print ic.label print sp
        DEVICE ID /.recordplays get exec num print sp
        ic.marks num print nl
    } if
} bind def

(few) 4 imagecase
(many) 60 imagecase
